With respect to the drowning issue, here are some suggestions from one who
is generally very, very pleased with the way the game works, but has
always been unhappy with pools.

First, I assume that a character's carrying capacity is related to
his/her strength, and possibly other factors; define a "sinking rate"
for the character proportional to the percentage of their capacity
that they are actually using, similar to the "encumbrance" measured
used in D&D.  Then a character with an empty inventory and no gold
would float, a lightly encumbered character could easily swim (maybe
use DEX as a modifier here), and a fully loaded character would begin
to sink rather rapidly.

Next, make death my drowning more like cockatrice stoning; i.e., it
could take more than one turn, and give you a chance to save yourself:

  You fall into the pool of water. -more-
  You start to sink rapidly
  <player types a d>
  Drop what? $
  You drop 4000 gold pieces. -more-
  You sink more slowly. -more-
  The water starts to tickle your nose.
  <player removes plate mail>
  You're having a hard time keeping your head above water.
  <player drops plate mail>
  Your plate mail sinks into the depths. -more-
  You rise to the surface.

I think this would still keep players on their toes, as the prospect
of loosing one's valuable armor should be a carelessness deterrent.
Nevertheless, it is easily preferable to sudden, irrevocable death
(modulo life-saving).

Third, pools could be of varying depths, with different effects for
the different types:

  You step into the shallow pool. -more-
  Your feet get wet.
  <player walks out of pool>
  Your elven boots squish noisily.

Etc. A pool would have various probabilities for being a certain
depth, with the chance of a pool being deeper increasing as the
character descends further into the dungeon. The moat, of course,
should be of the very deepest type. Shallow pools might contain
recoverable items. I don't think it should be possible to determine
the depth of a pool just by looking; as someone suggested, the
water probably isn't very clear (although this isn't obviously true in
the case of pools newly created by fountains; if the fountain water is
clear enough to drink, where would all the gunk come from in one turn).

Fourth, as I think someone else suggested, have the pools act as rust
traps, but have them affect the character's _entire_ inventory of
metal items (i.e., not just what is being worn/wielded). If
implemented in conjunction with the ideas above, the items affected
would be determined by pool depth, and length of immersion. A possible
order for rusting: boots, mail, shield, pack items, weapon (you'd keep
your arm above water, if you could), helmet. 

Finally, add more magic items related to water, besides water walking
boots. For example:
  Ring of flotation (lowers your sinking rate; cursed would raise it);
  Vest of buoyancy (AC 0, worn like a cloak, regular life vest);
  Oilcloth sack (keeps items inside from rusting);
  etc., etc.. 
You might find the vest underneath a throne (your seat cushion can be
used as a flotation device).

I understand that most of these would not be trivial changes to make.
This puts them clearly into the realm of 3.1, or beyond. Nevertheless,
I believe that these changes would enhance the game's playability, and
maybe even make it more FUN. Also, please note that most of the above
suggestions are independent of one another: sinking rate could be
replaced by a more easily computable boolean attribute "floats" or
somesuch, and that's all that's really for the other ideas.

The game is great anyway. Do with this what you will. Please email
flames to save bandwidth.

--John

